{
  "packet_id": "d17b9a098f17f5eb",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "A1 separates the objective (whether to invest, based on demand, willingness to pay and advantage) from the suggested solution. It lists real alternatives, including no expansion and licensing, and states the budget and timeline constraints. It does not identify the constraint a good analyst would not want missed: Ansell already exited retail household gloves. A1 instead treats consumer reach as simply 'unknown'. No false premise is corrected."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "T1 (A9), the best theory, names a benefit: fashion positioning leads to multiple purchases and premium pricing. The 'why' is a slogan-like assertion with no account of which consumers change behaviour or why they would choose an industrial glove maker. Value capture assumes brand pull and licensing royalties with no supporting reasoning. The chain to value this manufacturer captures is not traced."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "T1 (lifestyle/fashion) and T2 (medical necessity) nominally target different customers. However, T2 is built from the same template: identical assumptions and the same 'multiple pairs, premium pricing, licensing' value capture. Neither theory considers the rival that Ansell lacks consumer capability or that there is no demand, so the set is only weakly differentiated."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "The predictions are observable (higher purchase intent than an unbranded glove; stated willingness to buy multiple pairs). They rely on stated intent, so failure would only weakly threaten the theories. One assumption is asserted without support: that consumers 'currently view gloves as purely functional'."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 0,
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A12", "A14", "A15", "A16"],
      "reason": "Sources are misclassified. ansell-erj-marigold-sale is a verified observation but A15 labels it 'independent_study'. ansell-fool-fy26-segments is a verified observation but A12 and A16 label it 'company_assertion'. Every assessment is 'neutral', including the discontinued retail household business (A11, A14) and the sale of the Marigold consumer business (A15). Both bear directly on whether Ansell has a consumer capability to leverage. The grocer-partnership source, which says FHCS supplied the consumer research, marketing and sales, is never assessed. Key evidence is ignored or misread."
    },
    "seeking_challenge": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A13"],
      "reason": "A13 runs a single challenge search against T1 and retrieves sources (Marigold sale, grocer partnership, discontinuation). It never reports what they imply against T1. Their implications for Ansell's consumer capability go unexamined, so the challenge is nominal."
    },
    "experiment_design": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A17"],
      "reason": "The test is internally inconsistent. Both predictions in A17 compare against an unbranded functional arm, but the decision rule compares only lifestyle against medical. Even a 'T1 wins' result could therefore occur with no absolute demand. The thresholds overlap ('≥15' and '≤15'). Stated intent from 300 panelists cannot establish the purchase behaviour that A20 says is needed. The €20,000 cost and sample size are assumptions. The design only partially separates T1 from T2 and does not address the no-expansion alternative."
    },
